            "Robustness refers to whether a particular finding is replicable across a variety of settings, persons, and historical contexts, and some findings appear fragile, obtainable only under highly controlled conditions in a specific context, whereas others hold up despite significant variations in the conditions under which they are tested.",
            "Technically, robustness is demonstrated if a research study is conducted with a random sample of participants from a broadly defined population in a random sampling of settings, but such designs are usually impractical and not cost-effective, so this form of generalizability is more often established by repeated replications in systematically sampled settings and types of research participants.",
            "Critics argue that the restriction of populations and settings characteristic of laboratory research means that social psychology has a narrow database on which to draw conclusions about human nature, and historically the majority of experimental research has been conducted with the so-called WEIRD population of North American and Western European participants (Henrich et al., 2010; Sears, 1986).",
